<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Keōpūolani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1778–<a href="/years/1823">1823</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Keopuolani, Kalanikauikaʻalaneo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Keōpūolani was the highest-ranking wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a>, a chiefess of naha rank, born of a royal half-brother and half-sister marriage, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapu-hawaiian-culture">kapu</a> required commoners to fall prostrate before her. Daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiwalao">Kīwalaʻō</a>, king of Hawaiʻi island, she was caught with her grandmother Kalola after Kamehameha's victory at Kepaniwai on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> in 1790, and married him in 1795, linking his house to the ruling houses of Maui and Hawaiʻi. She bore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-ii">Kamehameha II</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a> and Princess Nāhiʻenaʻena. In 1819 she joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ka-ahumanu">Kaʻahumanu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hewahewa">Hewahewa</a> in sharing a meal of forbidden foods to break the kapu, and she was baptised a Christian just before her death in 1823.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/keopuolani.jpg" alt="Keōpūolani" width="330" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The American Tract Society · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Christian_Queen,_page_2_(1855)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Keōpūolani was born at Pahoehoe of Pāpōhaku, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wailuku">Wailuku</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a>, and was known in early childhood as Kalanikauikaʻalaneo; her later name means Gathering of the Clouds of Heaven. Her father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiwalao">Kīwalaʻō</a> was the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaniopuu">Kalaniʻōpuʻu</a> of Hawaiʻi island, and her mother, Kekuʻiapoiwa Liliha, a half-sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a>, shared a mother with Kīwalaʻō: High Chiefess Kalola of Maui. She lived for a time at Hāna before returning to Wailuku.</p><p>When Kamehameha defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalanikupule">Kalanikupule</a> at Kepaniwai in 1790, while her great-uncle King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahekili-ii">Kahekili II</a> was on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a>, Kalola fled with her family towards Oʻahu, but sickness halted them on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molokai">Molokaʻi</a>. Kalola offered her granddaughter as a future bride, and recognition of Kamehameha as ruler of Maui, in exchange for peace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Her rank gave her unquestioned social and political influence, and the marriage strengthened Kamehameha's claim after he had overthrown her father, his cousin. When chanters spoke her name, listeners bared themselves above the waist, and even Kamehameha removed his malo in her presence. She kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapu-hawaiian-culture">kapu</a> strictly but was mild to those who broke it, and they often fled to her for protection. Perhaps eleven or twelve children were born to her, of whom only three survived; her sons were raised by others, but, breaking the custom of hānai, she kept her daughter Nāhiʻenaʻena by her side.</p><p>After Kamehameha's death in 1819 she mostly stayed out of politics but generally supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ka-ahumanu">Kaʻahumanu</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuhina-nui">Kuhina Nui</a>. She married High Chief Hoapili, who carried Kamehameha's remains by canoe to a secret place on the Kona coast. After the missionaries arrived in 1820 she was among the first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alii">aliʻi</a> to become Christian, adopted western clothing and learned to read and write, and in 1823 she declared that royalty would no longer take several spouses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>She fell ill in August 1823. The missionary William Ellis spoke for the church at her baptism, and she took the Christian name of Harriet Stewart, wife of the missionary Charles Stewart. She died in the early evening of 16 September 1823 and was buried after a public funeral at Hale Kamani in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahaina">Lahaina</a>. In 1837 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a> moved her body to the sacred island of Mokuʻula, and her remains were perhaps later reburied at Waiola Church.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Keōpūolani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the death of Kamehameha the Great that year, two of his wives, Kaʻahumanu and <b>Keōpūolani</b>, then the most powerful people in the kingdom, conferred with the kahuna nui Hewahewa.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hawaiian-religion">Hawaiian religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Rank came from both parents: a chief’s children by a higher-born wife outranked him, and Kamehameha I lay on his back to let his children by the sacred <b>Keōpūolani</b> sit on his chest.</blockquote><a href="/societies/hawaii">Social Hierarchy of Hawaii under the Kapu</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wailuku">Wailuku</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahaina">Lahaina</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.89,-156.5&amp;year=1807" aria-label="Wailuku on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="340 1391 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Keōpūolani"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M790 1661h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M790 1661h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=20.89,-156.5&amp;year=1807">What was here in 1807? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
